From: the SK-D840N 7.3 line
Subject: [PATCH] net: zx279133: publish the TX fault isolation's counters

The TX fault isolation keeps four counters: how often a completion
counter could not be trusted, how often a recovery was asked for, how
often the shared datapath was restarted, and how often the recovery
failed.  Nothing reads them.  They are written under the TX lock and
never looked at, so on the board there is no way to tell a ring whose
counter was untrusted from a ring that never had a problem - which is
exactly the question the isolation exists to answer.

The 7.3 tree already has the place to publish them: the driver has an
ethtool statistics table, and the names in it are what "ethtool -S"
prints.  This patch adds the four counters to that table, at the end of
it so that the indices of the existing ones do not move, and reads them
under the same lock that protects them.

The names are the counters' own names, and they are added to the enum
and to the table in the same order: a name that is in one and not the
other would print a counter under the wrong label, and nothing in the
build would say so.

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c
index e367ad5..3e9f861 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c
@@ -1484,6 +1484,10 @@ enum zx279133_ethtool_stat {
 	ZX279133_STAT_XDP_TX,
 	ZX279133_STAT_XDP_REDIRECT,
 	ZX279133_STAT_XDP_ABORTED,
+	ZX279133_STAT_TX_INVALID_COMPLETIONS,
+	ZX279133_STAT_TX_RECOVERY_REQUESTS,
+	ZX279133_STAT_TX_RECOVERY_RESTARTS,
+	ZX279133_STAT_TX_RECOVERY_FAILURES,
 	ZX279133_STAT_COUNT,
 };
 
@@ -1555,6 +1559,10 @@ static const char zx279133_gstrings_stats[ZX279133_STAT_COUNT][ETH_GSTRING_LEN]
 	"xdp_tx",
 	"xdp_redirect",
 	"xdp_aborted",
+	"tx_invalid_completions",
+	"tx_recovery_requests",
+	"tx_recovery_restarts",
+	"tx_recovery_failures",
 };
 
 static u32 zx279133_hw_stat(struct zx279133_eth *eth, unsigned int offset)
@@ -1657,6 +1665,17 @@ static void zx279133_get_ethtool_stats(struct net_device *ndev,
 	data[ZX279133_STAT_TX1_COMPLETED] = eth->tx[1].completed;
 	data[ZX279133_STAT_TX_HW_CSUM_PACKETS] = eth->tx_hw_csum_packets;
 	data[ZX279133_STAT_TX_SW_CSUM_PACKETS] = eth->tx_sw_csum_packets;
+	/*
+	 * The TX fault isolation's own counters.  They say how often a
+	 * completion counter could not be trusted, and what the recovery
+	 * did about it.  They are read under the lock that protects them,
+	 * because they are incremented under it.
+	 */
+	data[ZX279133_STAT_TX_INVALID_COMPLETIONS] =
+		eth->tx_invalid_completions;
+	data[ZX279133_STAT_TX_RECOVERY_REQUESTS] = eth->tx_recovery_requests;
+	data[ZX279133_STAT_TX_RECOVERY_RESTARTS] = eth->tx_recovery_restarts;
+	data[ZX279133_STAT_TX_RECOVERY_FAILURES] = eth->tx_recovery_failures;
 	spin_unlock_bh(&eth->tx_lock);
 	data[ZX279133_STAT_RX_IRQ_COUNT] = atomic64_read(&eth->rx_irq_count);
 	data[ZX279133_STAT_IDM_LOCAL_IRQ_COUNT] =
